## Before any restic command

restic needs `RESTIC_REPOSITORY` and `RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE` (and any
backend credentials) in the environment. Source the env file once at
the start of your root shell:

```bash
set -a
. {{ restic_config_dir }}/env
set +a
```

After that, plain `restic snapshots`, `restic ls latest`, etc. work.
(The scheduled jobs source it the same way — no separate config to
keep in sync.)

## See what's scheduled

{% if restic_scheduler == "systemd" %}
```bash
systemctl list-timers 'restic-*'                        # next/last fire times
systemctl status restic-backup.timer                    # one specific timer
journalctl --since '24 hours ago' -u 'restic-*'         # recent runs
journalctl -fu restic-backup.service                    # follow a live backup
```
{% else %}
```bash
cat /etc/cron.d/restic
tail -F {{ restic_log_dir }}/*.log                      # follow live output
ls -lt {{ restic_log_dir }}/                            # most recent first
```
{% endif %}

## Run an ad-hoc backup right now

Useful before a risky change so you have a known-good snapshot to
recover from.

{% if restic_scheduler == "systemd" %}
```bash
systemctl start restic-backup.service
journalctl -fu restic-backup.service                    # watch it run
```

The scheduled timer is unaffected — its next fire time stays where it was.
{% else %}
```bash
{{ restic_config_dir }}/restic-backup.sh
```
{% endif %}

## Inspect what got backed up

```bash
restic snapshots                       # list all snapshots
restic snapshots --latest 3            # just the three most recent
restic snapshots --host {{ inventory_hostname }}     # filter to this host (multi-host repos)
restic stats latest                    # size & file count of the latest snapshot
restic stats --mode raw-data           # raw repo size

restic ls latest                       # full file tree in latest snapshot
restic ls latest /etc                  # one subtree
restic ls --long latest /etc           # with sizes / mtimes
restic find PATTERN                    # search snapshots by filename

restic diff <old-id> <new-id>          # what changed between two snapshots
```

Snapshot IDs are the short hex strings shown by `restic snapshots`. The
literal word `latest` always works as an alias for the newest snapshot.

## Verify integrity

```bash
restic check                              # fast — metadata only
restic check --read-data-subset=10%       # slower — catches on-disk bit-rot
```

The role already runs the metadata check on the `restic-check` schedule
and the data-subset check on the `restic-check-data` schedule. Ad-hoc
runs are most useful **after** a suspected hardware issue or **before**
relying on a snapshot to recover something important.

## Restore data

### Step 1 — preview the restore (writes nothing)

**Always do this first.** It prints exactly which files would be
written and where, with no changes to disk:

```bash
restic restore latest --target /tmp/restore-preview --dry-run --verbose=2
```

Add `--include /path/under/snapshot` or `--exclude PATTERN` to narrow.

### Step 2 — restore to a sandbox

Never restore directly on top of live data. Restore into a fresh
directory, inspect, then copy what you actually need into place.

```bash
mkdir -p /var/restore
restic restore latest --target /var/restore
ls /var/restore                                    # contents mirror the original tree
```

### Restore just one path

```bash
restic restore latest --target /var/restore --include /etc/somefile.conf
# the file lands at /var/restore/etc/somefile.conf
```

### Restore from a specific (non-latest) snapshot

```bash
restic snapshots                                   # find the id you want
restic restore <snapshot-id> --target /var/restore
```

### Pull a single file as a stream (no temp tree)

```bash
restic dump latest /etc/somefile.conf > /tmp/somefile.conf
```

### Browse the repository like a filesystem (read-only)

```bash
apt-get install -y fuse                            # if not already present
mkdir -p /mnt/restic
restic mount /mnt/restic &                         # mounts /mnt/restic/snapshots/
ls /mnt/restic/snapshots/latest/
# ... browse / cp / diff freely ...
fusermount -u /mnt/restic                          # when done
```

## Test that restore actually works (run periodically)

A backup that has never been restored is a backup that might not work.
Quick round-trip test that touches no live files:

```bash
TMP=$(mktemp -d /tmp/restic-test.XXXXXX)
restic restore latest --target "$TMP" --include /etc/hostname
diff /etc/hostname "$TMP/etc/hostname" && echo "RESTORE OK"
rm -rf "$TMP"
```

Pick a different small file each time so you exercise more of the
restore path over time.

## Preview retention before it deletes anything

The scheduled `restic-forget` job applies the retention policy
(keep-daily {{ restic_keep_daily }}, keep-weekly {{ restic_keep_weekly }}, keep-monthly {{ restic_keep_monthly }}, keep-yearly {{ restic_keep_yearly }}). To see what *would* be removed without doing it:

```bash
restic forget --dry-run \
  --keep-daily {{ restic_keep_daily }} --keep-weekly {{ restic_keep_weekly }} \
  --keep-monthly {{ restic_keep_monthly }} --keep-yearly {{ restic_keep_yearly }}
```

## Common problems

### "repository is already locked"

A previous restic process left a stale lock (host rebooted mid-run,
SIGKILL, etc). Confirm nothing live is holding it, then clear:

```bash
{% if restic_scheduler == "systemd" %}
systemctl list-units 'restic-*' --state=running    # should be empty
{% endif %}
restic list locks
restic unlock                                       # only if no live process
```

### "Fatal: wrong password ..."

Verify the password file is intact:

```bash
sha256sum {{ restic_password_file }}
```

If the password is lost, **the repository is unrecoverable** — there is
no maintainer backdoor by design. The role can copy the password back
to the Ansible control node on apply; see `restic_fetch_password_to_control`
in the role README.

### Need to see exactly what the scheduled jobs run

The job scripts are plain bash and safe to read:

```bash
ls -l {{ restic_config_dir }}/restic-*.sh
cat {{ restic_config_dir }}/restic-backup.sh
```

